WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:07.733
- Our next speaker is Judith Litchman,

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:10.760
who is Susan Dwight Bliss Professor

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.480
and Chair of Chronic Disease Epidemiology

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:15.090
at Yale School of Public Health

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:17.240
and Director of the Humanities Arts

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:19.140
and Public Health Practice at Yale

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:21.130
or HAPPY Initiative.

00:00:21.130 --> 00:00:22.860
Dr. Lichtman's research focuses

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.850
on examining the epidemiology of stroke

00:00:24.850 --> 00:00:27.235
and heart disease using
longitudinal databases

00:00:27.235 --> 00:00:29.870
and prospective observational studies.

00:00:29.870 --> 00:00:32.160
She has served on several
national committees related

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:33.280
to cardiovascular

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.310
and cerebrovascular disorders

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.070
and has been a member
of the program committee

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:38.858
for the American Heart
Association conference

00:00:38.858 --> 00:00:41.090
on cardiovascular disease,

00:00:41.090 --> 00:00:43.330
epidemiology and prevention,

00:00:43.330 --> 00:00:44.163
and has served

00:00:44.163 --> 00:00:45.820
on numerous national
American Heart Association

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:48.740
and American Stroke
Association writing committees.

00:00:48.740 --> 00:00:49.573
She was the recipient

00:00:49.573 --> 00:00:53.110
of the C Miller Fisher, MD
Neuroscience Visionary Award

00:00:53.110 --> 00:00:56.370
from the AHA and the ASA in 2018

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:57.203
and is a graduate

00:00:57.203 --> 00:00:59.070
of the Drexel University
Executive Leadership

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.620
in Academic Medicine program.

00:01:01.620 --> 00:01:03.710
Dr. Lichtman has been the
principal investigator

00:01:03.710 --> 00:01:05.810
for projects funded by
the Goddess Foundation,

00:01:05.810 --> 00:01:07.790
the Fannie E. Rippel Foundation,

00:01:07.790 --> 00:01:09.330
the American Heart Association,

00:01:09.330 --> 00:01:11.470
the Centers for Disease
Control and Prevention,

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:13.210
National Institute on Aging,

00:01:13.210 --> 00:01:15.600
the Agency for Healthcare
Research and Quality,

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:18.070
the National Heart Lung
and Blood Institute

00:01:18.070 --> 00:01:20.670
and the National Institute
of Neurological Disorders

00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:21.653
and Stroke.

00:01:22.700 --> 00:01:23.533
- All right, well, thank you.

00:01:23.533 --> 00:01:24.590
So I have to say,

00:01:24.590 --> 00:01:26.790
I mean, it's wonderful to,

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:28.800
to revisit some of those accomplishments,

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.640
but I wanna say I'm a graduate of the,

00:01:31.640 --> 00:01:32.830
of the program here.

00:01:32.830 --> 00:01:36.680
And so I have to say my
area was in epidemiology.

00:01:36.680 --> 00:01:40.630
I have been living the past
little while across the bridge,

00:01:40.630 --> 00:01:42.150
over in the school of public health

00:01:42.150 --> 00:01:43.631
and around the medical center.

00:01:43.631 --> 00:01:45.840
And what's really fun about coming today

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.490
and hearing that incredible project is

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.460
that I've always had roots in music

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:52.370
and dance

00:01:52.370 --> 00:01:53.910
and art.

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.830
And I sort of feel like I'm enjoying

00:01:56.830 --> 00:01:58.840
a treasure hunt across campus,

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:00.160
finding out about,

00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:02.380
and really trying to build something that,

00:02:02.380 --> 00:02:05.020
that goes beyond sort of my area,

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:07.610
but to really extend
across the different arts

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:08.560
and disciplines.

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:10.340
So what I wanna do today
is talk a little bit

00:02:10.340 --> 00:02:13.050
about the vision of what
it is I'm trying to build.

00:02:13.050 --> 00:02:16.000
And we're in process
share with you a course

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:16.880
and a new course

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:18.060
that's coming out this spring

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:19.550
that we're really excited about.

00:02:19.550 --> 00:02:20.900
And then just tell you about one

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:21.733
or two projects.

00:02:21.733 --> 00:02:22.566
And again,

00:02:22.566 --> 00:02:24.120
I'm very interested in hearing

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.800
about potential collaborations
or opportunities,

00:02:26.800 --> 00:02:27.760
cause it's really exciting

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:29.040
to see where you are in your,

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:30.700
your own career development.

00:02:30.700 --> 00:02:34.410
And I have to say Yale
is an amazing space.

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:35.243
As I said,

00:02:35.243 --> 00:02:36.160
I've been here a little while

00:02:36.160 --> 00:02:38.700
and I'm still exploring new territory.

00:02:38.700 --> 00:02:39.893
So it's a lot of fun.

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:44.160
So what we really wanna do
with the HAPPY Initiative is,

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:46.450
is develop an interdisciplinary program

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.100
that really builds on health,

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:50.170
the arts and humanities,

00:02:50.170 --> 00:02:53.410
looking at all of the rich
resources across Yale.

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:55.580
We're basing it in the
school of public health.

00:02:55.580 --> 00:02:56.440
That's my hub.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:57.520
And we really are giving

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.880
it more of a public health perspective,

00:02:59.880 --> 00:03:02.060
how to enhance the health of communities

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:06.060
through understanding
important public health issues,

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:07.400
but how can we translate it

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:09.223
and communicate it through the arts?

00:03:10.210 --> 00:03:13.620
And so one of the things we have sort

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.010
of a three-pronged approach,

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:16.640
we wanna have collaboration,

00:03:16.640 --> 00:03:18.790
which is what's exciting
here to sort of see

00:03:18.790 --> 00:03:21.690
what everybody's doing to
really build collaborations,

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:24.100
to eventually establish a center

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:25.470
that focuses on this

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:28.410
with certainly invited scholars,

00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:29.640
having guests artists,

00:03:29.640 --> 00:03:30.570
and really having

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:33.780
a good hub for these
kinds of conversations.

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:35.630
We also wanna have education

00:03:35.630 --> 00:03:40.490
to really train everybody to
use interdisciplinary arts

00:03:40.490 --> 00:03:42.840
to promote community health.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:46.190
And we are working on a
track within my school,

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:48.840
but also thinking across campus.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:50.660
And then research

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.040
that's as you heard a bit of my hub is

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:54.350
to really conduct research

00:03:54.350 --> 00:03:55.320
that evaluates

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:57.610
and comes up with how to measure outcomes

00:03:57.610 --> 00:03:59.440
of art-based programming.

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:01.310
So how can we improve community health

00:04:01.310 --> 00:04:03.880
and prove that a lot of this exposure

00:04:03.880 --> 00:04:07.000
is really going to improve
an individual's health

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:10.620
and the overall health of their community.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:11.780
So one of the things,

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:15.510
and I'm sure many of you are
from different areas here,

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:17.190
part of what is really fun.

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:18.023
And as I said,

00:04:18.023 --> 00:04:21.040
the treasure hunt is to sort
of build collaborations.

00:04:21.040 --> 00:04:22.890
And we've been doing
that very effectively.

00:04:22.890 --> 00:04:25.070
So far we've been
working with individuals.

00:04:25.070 --> 00:04:26.030
I should change it now

00:04:26.030 --> 00:04:28.910
from School of Drama to the Geffen School.

00:04:28.910 --> 00:04:30.520
So it's a little bit older, sorry,

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:32.000
but what is interesting is,

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:32.833
you know, again,

00:04:32.833 --> 00:04:34.730
how many schools are under our umbrella,

00:04:34.730 --> 00:04:37.610
but also we think about all
of the other art galleries,

00:04:37.610 --> 00:04:39.260
museums, libraries,

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:42.300
everything else that are just
so rich to Yale to be thinking

00:04:42.300 --> 00:04:44.740
about how can we incorporate programs

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:45.870
and conversations

00:04:45.870 --> 00:04:49.210
that really bridge
across these disciplines?

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:50.700
One of the collaborations

00:04:50.700 --> 00:04:51.690
that we're really excited

00:04:51.690 --> 00:04:53.560
about is we've been
working with Garth Ross

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:54.760
and Jennifer Newman,

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:57.200
thinking about using

00:04:57.200 --> 00:04:58.960
and working with the Schwarzman Center.

00:04:58.960 --> 00:04:59.910
For me, as I said,

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:02.430
sort of being over in
the more medical campus,

00:05:02.430 --> 00:05:04.340
it really is a wonderful environment.

00:05:04.340 --> 00:05:05.910
I'm sure many of you
have already been there.

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:08.470
You've seen the beautiful new resources,

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:10.803
but it's amazing that the dance space,

00:05:12.417 --> 00:05:14.530
all the different areas and technologies

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:15.980
that we're hoping to take advantage

00:05:15.980 --> 00:05:19.410
of to really immerse
ourselves in thematic work

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:22.120
and also display so many
of the different arts

00:05:22.120 --> 00:05:23.163
that are available.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:26.740
And it really provides a
unique lens into public health

00:05:26.740 --> 00:05:29.491
and the arts by being
able to have conversations

00:05:29.491 --> 00:05:30.410
that bridge.

00:05:30.410 --> 00:05:31.250
And I'll talk about one

00:05:31.250 --> 00:05:32.590
or two of those.

00:05:32.590 --> 00:05:35.670
Taiga Christie, who was a
graduate of our MPH program

00:05:35.670 --> 00:05:38.710
and the social behavioral sciences is what

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:41.240
is an inaugural Schwartzman fellow.

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:43.230
What that means is it's
really exciting for us.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:45.940
I mean, she knows the
disciplines of our work,

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:48.770
but she also is really having
important conversations

00:05:48.770 --> 00:05:51.700
and introductions for us
within the Schwarzman Center,

00:05:51.700 --> 00:05:52.556
to be able to think

00:05:52.556 --> 00:05:56.650
about developing programs and events

00:05:56.650 --> 00:06:00.000
that are dedicated to this intersection

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:04.493
and can also introduce
individuals across campus.

00:06:07.290 --> 00:06:09.080
We feel that we're also helpful

00:06:09.080 --> 00:06:11.480
to the Schwarzman Center's mission

00:06:11.480 --> 00:06:13.010
of building a sense of belonging

00:06:13.010 --> 00:06:16.200
by providing some of
these unique opportunities

00:06:16.200 --> 00:06:17.880
to discover and connect.

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:18.800
And one of the themes

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:21.230
that we're working on right
now is a theme of sleep.

00:06:21.230 --> 00:06:22.490
We all need sleep.

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:23.760
It's one of what we call sort of

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:26.410
the three pillars of a stool.

00:06:26.410 --> 00:06:27.740
You know, you need to eat,

00:06:27.740 --> 00:06:29.850
you need to exercise.

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:31.960
You also need to sleep.

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:34.160
And what's fun about this
is we've been starting

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:35.240
to have conversations

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:37.500
where we've been bringing
together the experts in sleep,

00:06:37.500 --> 00:06:38.333
who can talk about

00:06:38.333 --> 00:06:40.850
what you do when you
sleep brain cleansing,

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:41.790
circadian rhythms,

00:06:41.790 --> 00:06:44.310
all the things that are really
important for your health.

00:06:44.310 --> 00:06:45.540
We're also talking with,

00:06:45.540 --> 00:06:46.373
for example,

00:06:46.373 --> 00:06:48.990
individuals from the
Geffen School of Drama,

00:06:48.990 --> 00:06:50.800
where we're talking about safety issues,

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:51.633
shift work,

00:06:51.633 --> 00:06:52.710
how does that work?

00:06:52.710 --> 00:06:54.980
We're also talking with individuals

00:06:54.980 --> 00:06:56.510
about equity of sleep,

00:06:56.510 --> 00:06:58.640
who has good sleeping environments.

00:06:58.640 --> 00:07:00.140
We're working with professors

00:07:00.140 --> 00:07:01.970
and individuals from the
school of architecture,

00:07:01.970 --> 00:07:04.060
what is the ideal sleeping environment?

00:07:04.060 --> 00:07:07.510
So we are starting to
think about not only the,

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:10.040
the broad range of importance of sleep

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:11.250
and how well it's connected.

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:14.720
We're thinking about plays and music,

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:16.860
all that have showcased
the importance of sleep

00:07:16.860 --> 00:07:19.080
and the detrimental issues related

00:07:19.080 --> 00:07:20.700
to not having adequate sleep.

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:21.533
And of course,

00:07:21.533 --> 00:07:22.366
for all of us,

00:07:22.366 --> 00:07:24.300
whether it be a graduate student level

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:25.310
or faculty level,

00:07:25.310 --> 00:07:26.143
we know there,

00:07:26.143 --> 00:07:27.550
there are important times

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:29.030
when we are not getting enough sleep.

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:31.610
And we have to think about
how that impacts health.

00:07:31.610 --> 00:07:33.360
So stay tuned,

00:07:33.360 --> 00:07:34.740
we're planning quite a few events

00:07:34.740 --> 00:07:35.660
that we will be bringing

00:07:35.660 --> 00:07:37.540
across campus to really think about

00:07:37.540 --> 00:07:38.653
that and showcase it.

00:07:39.700 --> 00:07:41.710
I'm also really excited when we think

00:07:41.710 --> 00:07:44.650
about this kind of approach,

00:07:44.650 --> 00:07:47.440
we have a incredible collaborator

00:07:47.440 --> 00:07:49.680
that I was fortunate to be
introduced to about a year

00:07:49.680 --> 00:07:50.513
and a half ago,

00:07:50.513 --> 00:07:51.440
Dr. Neal baer,

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:53.130
he's a physician, author,

00:07:53.130 --> 00:07:54.780
an award-winning show runner

00:07:54.780 --> 00:07:56.790
and television writer/producer.

00:07:56.790 --> 00:07:58.470
He is very passionate

00:07:58.470 --> 00:08:00.800
about public health being an advocate,

00:08:00.800 --> 00:08:04.270
and he really uses media
to tell impactful stories.

00:08:04.270 --> 00:08:06.470
And so he works from the approach

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:10.060
that you need to have it
based in science and evidence,

00:08:10.060 --> 00:08:13.420
but then you really have to
trigger an emotional response

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:15.200
to things it's not just about the numbers,

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:18.991
but how do you really invoke
the emotional response

00:08:18.991 --> 00:08:21.310
to really wanna drive change

00:08:21.310 --> 00:08:22.850
and end policy?

00:08:22.850 --> 00:08:23.683
So again,

00:08:23.683 --> 00:08:26.620
I would, his list is very long,

00:08:26.620 --> 00:08:28.450
but again, I think for us,

00:08:28.450 --> 00:08:30.130
it's really exciting to be working

00:08:30.130 --> 00:08:31.960
with someone who is very visionary

00:08:31.960 --> 00:08:34.210
and really wants to
connect all of the dots

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:36.620
and think about effective
storytelling because that,

00:08:36.620 --> 00:08:39.300
for us as a public health discipline

00:08:39.300 --> 00:08:42.350
and through many of the other
arts is really important

00:08:42.350 --> 00:08:43.800
and compelling.

00:08:43.800 --> 00:08:45.370
I also wanna mention Karen Derry,

00:08:45.370 --> 00:08:46.203
who's here.

00:08:46.203 --> 00:08:48.130
Who's really the hub of
making sure we connect

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:50.010
with everybody across campus.

00:08:50.010 --> 00:08:50.843
So let me just,

00:08:50.843 --> 00:08:53.480
so that's a collaboration education.

00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:54.680
We really are trying to think

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:57.210
about multidisciplinary courses

00:08:57.210 --> 00:08:58.360
that take advantage

00:08:58.360 --> 00:09:01.290
of understanding public health issues

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:03.020
from the perspective of the science,

00:09:03.020 --> 00:09:04.970
as well as how they might be displayed

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:06.560
to have been historically

00:09:06.560 --> 00:09:09.890
and how they might be used
to really raise awareness

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:12.330
and invoke change.

00:09:12.330 --> 00:09:14.880
So I wanna talk about my
Inaugural HAPPY Course

00:09:14.880 --> 00:09:15.713
in just a minute,

00:09:15.713 --> 00:09:16.546
but that's the,

00:09:16.546 --> 00:09:19.660
as I said, collaboration, education,

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:20.500
and then research,

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:23.870
we really wanna build an
evidence base for ethical,

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:24.980
practical use of arts

00:09:24.980 --> 00:09:26.800
and humanities to improve health outcomes.

00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:27.950
It seems obvious,

00:09:27.950 --> 00:09:30.430
but there really is an
important gap in the field.

00:09:30.430 --> 00:09:32.990
And what's really exciting
from my perspective is

00:09:32.990 --> 00:09:36.155
that organizations like
the National Institutes

00:09:36.155 --> 00:09:39.030
of Health are really
recognizing this importance.

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:41.710
And there are now requests
for funding announcements.

00:09:41.710 --> 00:09:42.543
For example,

00:09:42.543 --> 00:09:44.590
I'm working with Dean Robert Blocker

00:09:44.590 --> 00:09:45.840
from the School of Music.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:48.650
There are new RFAs requests
for funding announcements

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:52.100
that are very much related
to brain health music,

00:09:52.100 --> 00:09:54.580
all aspects of recovery as mentioned,

00:09:54.580 --> 00:09:55.850
I'm very interested in stroke.

00:09:55.850 --> 00:09:57.750
So stroke recovery is something

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:01.310
that's very interesting to
me to understand how music

00:10:01.310 --> 00:10:03.160
and dance are very,

00:10:03.160 --> 00:10:05.420
very helpful in recovery.

00:10:05.420 --> 00:10:07.240
And then part of what's important.

00:10:07.240 --> 00:10:09.160
And I've heard this from Garth,

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:10.520
from Schwartzman Center,

00:10:10.520 --> 00:10:12.350
who's spent a lot of time down

00:10:12.350 --> 00:10:14.970
at the Kennedy Center
for Arts is we also need

00:10:14.970 --> 00:10:17.700
to be thinking about how
do you evaluate the impact.

00:10:17.700 --> 00:10:20.870
And there really is a lot of
work methodologically to do.

00:10:20.870 --> 00:10:22.460
So let me just take a couple of minutes

00:10:22.460 --> 00:10:24.270
and talk about the course
that we're planning.

00:10:24.270 --> 00:10:26.370
So this is a picture of
the historical library

00:10:26.370 --> 00:10:27.360
over at the medical school,

00:10:27.360 --> 00:10:30.000
which is gonna be very much
involved in our course.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:31.750
We really wanna challenge our students

00:10:31.750 --> 00:10:33.840
to explore the possibilities problems

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:35.690
and potential uses of arts

00:10:35.690 --> 00:10:37.650
and humanities in public health.

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:40.230
So we're planning field
trips all around the campus.

00:10:40.230 --> 00:10:44.010
We've already talked with
one of the art historians.

00:10:44.010 --> 00:10:47.020
We're going to pull out a lot of materials

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:50.390
that have been historically provided,

00:10:50.390 --> 00:10:53.130
whether it be how things
have been recorded,

00:10:53.130 --> 00:10:56.578
how things have been
relayed in advertisements,

00:10:56.578 --> 00:10:59.204
so that we really are
building conversations

00:10:59.204 --> 00:11:01.803
around particular themes.

00:11:02.830 --> 00:11:06.180
We wanna really look at
expertise across campus.

00:11:06.180 --> 00:11:07.750
I'm gonna show you
pictures of other places.

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:09.150
We're gonna go on our field trip.

00:11:09.150 --> 00:11:11.400
And we really wanna think
about methodologies,

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:14.130
not only study design
and things like that,

00:11:14.130 --> 00:11:16.700
but how do we really think

00:11:16.700 --> 00:11:19.430
about the impact of some of these things

00:11:19.430 --> 00:11:20.950
on our own perceptions

00:11:20.950 --> 00:11:22.850
and how will we utilize these to think

00:11:22.850 --> 00:11:24.923
about community health?

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:29.610
Here we expect to be
going to the libraries.

00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:32.630
I mean, the museums to really take a look

00:11:32.630 --> 00:11:35.980
at how some public health
themes have been relayed in art.

00:11:35.980 --> 00:11:37.780
And so the format,

00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:38.830
which is very different

00:11:38.830 --> 00:11:41.030
for me is we're gonna have sort

00:11:41.030 --> 00:11:42.230
of two sessions a week.

00:11:42.230 --> 00:11:44.960
One will be more lecture
oriented content area.

00:11:44.960 --> 00:11:46.760
The other one's gonna be very hands-on,

00:11:46.760 --> 00:11:48.170
it's gonna be going to,

00:11:48.170 --> 00:11:50.610
as I said, some of the libraries,

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:51.700
some of the museums,

00:11:51.700 --> 00:11:54.460
we have some concerts picked
out some performances,

00:11:54.460 --> 00:11:56.630
so that we're really trying to reflect

00:11:56.630 --> 00:11:59.890
on how these two are both integrated

00:11:59.890 --> 00:12:02.290
and sometimes not integrated.

00:12:02.290 --> 00:12:04.200
We're gonna really focus on ethics

00:12:04.200 --> 00:12:06.150
and problems in art and health work,

00:12:06.150 --> 00:12:08.560
storytelling, social cohesion,

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:09.890
and medical humanities,

00:12:09.890 --> 00:12:12.760
and social science to kind of
build this methologic base.

00:12:12.760 --> 00:12:14.560
And then we're really gonna be thinking

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:17.090
about what are some of the
important public health themes

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:18.600
that we wanna get across.

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:19.710
So some of them,

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:20.543
for example,

00:12:20.543 --> 00:12:21.980
might be things like climate change,

00:12:21.980 --> 00:12:24.780
disaster and humanitarian aid, opioids

00:12:24.780 --> 00:12:25.730
and harm reduction,

00:12:25.730 --> 00:12:27.700
violence and gun violence, sleep

00:12:27.700 --> 00:12:29.380
and health disparities,

00:12:29.380 --> 00:12:31.150
mental health, HIV aids,

00:12:31.150 --> 00:12:32.200
the built environment

00:12:32.200 --> 00:12:33.680
and health disparities,

00:12:33.680 --> 00:12:35.410
health impact of incarceration,

00:12:35.410 --> 00:12:36.990
which was wonderful to hear.

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:39.600
And we'll be working with
one of my former students,

00:12:39.600 --> 00:12:40.433
Byron Kennedy.

00:12:40.433 --> 00:12:42.930
I don't know if you've met him,

00:12:42.930 --> 00:12:44.450
who's very involved in,

00:12:44.450 --> 00:12:48.760
in the medical system for the
prison systems in Connecticut

00:12:48.760 --> 00:12:50.850
and then neuroscience and the arts.

00:12:50.850 --> 00:12:52.570
And I just wanna mention

00:12:52.570 --> 00:12:54.700
that part of this also
is to look at plays.

00:12:54.700 --> 00:12:58.360
This is actually a scene
from a play that was written,

00:12:58.360 --> 00:13:00.440
directed and produced by,

00:13:00.440 --> 00:13:01.273
as I mentioned,

00:13:01.273 --> 00:13:02.106
Taiga Christie,

00:13:02.106 --> 00:13:02.940
who is our fellow.

00:13:02.940 --> 00:13:04.560
And it was an important piece in

00:13:04.560 --> 00:13:08.880
that it really looked at the
stress of frontline workers,

00:13:08.880 --> 00:13:09.920
EMS workers.

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:12.150
She did a qualitative work

00:13:12.150 --> 00:13:15.260
to understand the issues
she developed a play,

00:13:15.260 --> 00:13:18.610
and then it was shown to EMS workers

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:19.460
and their families.

00:13:19.460 --> 00:13:21.540
And it led to very important discussions

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:23.123
about many of those issues.

00:13:24.840 --> 00:13:26.270
The assessments that we're gonna do,

00:13:26.270 --> 00:13:28.910
which is a little different
is weekly reading reflection.

00:13:28.910 --> 00:13:31.400
So we really want people to think

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:35.190
about their response to
a lot of these issues so

00:13:35.190 --> 00:13:38.210
that we really want people to come up

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:42.030
with where these things
connect in their own lives.

00:13:42.030 --> 00:13:45.170
We want individuals to create a manifesto,

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:46.440
sort of a value assignment.

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:48.710
That's a kind of a living document

00:13:48.710 --> 00:13:50.300
that will grow with the work

00:13:50.300 --> 00:13:51.133
that they do.

00:13:51.133 --> 00:13:53.000
And then we really want a final project,

00:13:53.000 --> 00:13:54.240
which is to create an arts

00:13:54.240 --> 00:13:56.840
or humanities inspired intervention

00:13:56.840 --> 00:13:58.900
on a specific health topic.

00:13:58.900 --> 00:14:00.510
I'm also showcasing here.

00:14:00.510 --> 00:14:01.720
We talked about music.

00:14:01.720 --> 00:14:03.430
This is one of our former students

00:14:03.430 --> 00:14:07.400
who also had a wonderful
series of recitals

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:08.543
during her time here.

00:14:10.360 --> 00:14:11.470
So that's the course,

00:14:11.470 --> 00:14:12.303
a couple of things

00:14:12.303 --> 00:14:13.290
that we have put together,

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:14.500
just to give you an idea,

00:14:14.500 --> 00:14:18.480
is we hosted Dr. Neal Baer

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.360
had been the executive
producer for a documentary

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.220
Welcome To Chechnya,

00:14:23.220 --> 00:14:27.060
which was a powerful
David France documentary

00:14:27.060 --> 00:14:32.060
about anti-gay purges in the
late 2010s for LGBT refugees.

00:14:34.130 --> 00:14:35.630
They were wearing hidden cameras,

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:36.640
made their way out of Russia

00:14:36.640 --> 00:14:38.130
through a network of safe houses.

00:14:38.130 --> 00:14:39.630
And it was really amazing.

00:14:39.630 --> 00:14:40.980
It was a nominated,

00:14:40.980 --> 00:14:41.860
it was on the shortlist,

00:14:41.860 --> 00:14:44.360
I should say for an Oscar,

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:47.360
it won a Special Jury at Sundance.

00:14:47.360 --> 00:14:49.040
What was interesting about it is

00:14:49.040 --> 00:14:50.370
that we put together a panel,

00:14:50.370 --> 00:14:51.800
which was Neal baer.

00:14:51.800 --> 00:14:54.911
We also had our Dean John Pachankis,

00:14:54.911 --> 00:14:58.533
whose really his area is an LBGTQ health.

00:14:59.490 --> 00:15:00.610
Lyosha Gorshkov,

00:15:00.610 --> 00:15:02.080
who's also in academics,

00:15:02.080 --> 00:15:05.560
but has been a refugee

00:15:05.560 --> 00:15:10.560
and also a lawyer Dierdre
Sradone who is an expert

00:15:10.840 --> 00:15:12.470
in getting people asylum.

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:13.640
So it was really,

00:15:13.640 --> 00:15:16.760
we provided the documentary.

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:18.370
We gave people time to look at it,

00:15:18.370 --> 00:15:20.190
and then we really put together a panel

00:15:20.190 --> 00:15:23.030
that we thought could get
at some of the issues,

00:15:23.030 --> 00:15:25.890
the social political issues

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:27.990
that we thought would be really important.

00:15:29.410 --> 00:15:31.940
We also, and it was
wonderful to see the readings

00:15:31.940 --> 00:15:32.860
that in the letters

00:15:32.860 --> 00:15:34.848
that you were describing.

00:15:34.848 --> 00:15:35.681
(clearing throat)

00:15:35.681 --> 00:15:37.100
We put together a pandemic archive,

00:15:37.100 --> 00:15:38.667
and it's been very interesting.

00:15:38.667 --> 00:15:42.380
We opened it up to the
El community and beyond,

00:15:42.380 --> 00:15:43.950
and got wonderful contributions

00:15:43.950 --> 00:15:46.310
about people's responses

00:15:46.310 --> 00:15:48.370
and impressions and thoughts

00:15:48.370 --> 00:15:51.260
about what their lives
were like during that time,

00:15:51.260 --> 00:15:52.570
which we think as,

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:55.490
as you were hearing is a
really important expression

00:15:55.490 --> 00:16:00.270
and a way for many people to
think about getting through

00:16:00.270 --> 00:16:02.380
what was such a difficult time.

00:16:02.380 --> 00:16:05.050
And then some other things we've done.

00:16:05.050 --> 00:16:08.440
We provided opportunity for,

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:10.040
we invited a group of students

00:16:10.040 --> 00:16:14.290
and staff across the whole
campus to view a play,

00:16:14.290 --> 00:16:15.910
Do No Harm.

00:16:15.910 --> 00:16:17.930
That was really around the stories

00:16:17.930 --> 00:16:19.670
of three enslaved black women

00:16:19.670 --> 00:16:21.780
and the medical experimentation

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:23.430
that they endured

00:16:23.430 --> 00:16:24.620
and suffered through.

00:16:24.620 --> 00:16:26.430
And following the screening,

00:16:26.430 --> 00:16:29.840
we had a conversation with the playwright

00:16:29.840 --> 00:16:31.320
and the play commissioner,

00:16:31.320 --> 00:16:34.840
and really had a very wonderful dialogue

00:16:34.840 --> 00:16:35.940
about many of the issues

00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:36.890
that were involved.

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:40.750
And then finally, we have a
couple of things in process.

00:16:40.750 --> 00:16:44.520
I mentioned Taiga Christie's
work Counting Pebbles,

00:16:44.520 --> 00:16:47.050
but we also are currently working

00:16:47.050 --> 00:16:49.860
and she's leading the
initiative on an original play

00:16:49.860 --> 00:16:53.310
